<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colin Renfrew</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1937">1937</a>–<a href="/years/2024">2024</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Andrew Colin Renfrew, Baron Renfrew of Kaimsthorn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Colin Renfrew, Baron Renfrew of Kaimsthorn, was a British archaeologist, paleolinguist and Conservative peer noted for his work on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radiocarbon-dating">radiocarbon dating</a>, the prehistory of languages, archaeogenetics, neuroarchaeology and the prevention of looting. In Before Civilisation (1973) he challenged the assumption that prehistoric cultural innovation originated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> and spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. His Anatolian hypothesis, set out in Archaeology and Language (1987), held that Proto-Indo-European originated about 9,000 years ago in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and spread with farming, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marija-gimbutas">Marija Gimbutas</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurgan">Kurgan</a> hypothesis. He was Disney Professor of Archaeology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> from 1981 to 2004.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colin-renfrew.jpg" alt="Colin Renfrew" width="330" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Portable Antiquities Scheme from London, England · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TVC_June_2018_(29165787578)_(Renfrew_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Andrew Colin Renfrew was born on 25 July 1937 and educated at St Albans School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hertfordshire">Hertfordshire</a>. From 1956 to 1958 he did National Service in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a>. He then went up to St John's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, where he read first Natural Sciences and then Archaeology and Anthropology, graduating in 1962. He was elected president of the Cambridge Union in 1961. In 1965 he completed his doctoral thesis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> cultures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a> and their external relations, and married Jane M. Ewbank in the same year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1965 Renfrew became a lecturer at the University of Sheffield, and between 1968 and 1970 he directed excavations at Sitagroi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, where he also worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marija-gimbutas">Marija Gimbutas</a>. In 1972 he became Professor of Archaeology at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southampton">Southampton</a>, succeeding Barry Cunliffe, and directed excavations at Quanterness in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phylakopi">Phylakopi</a> on the island of Milos. He was elected a Fellow of the British Academy in 1980 and in 1981 became Disney Professor of Archaeology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, a post he held until he retired in 2004.</p><p>He was Master of Jesus College from 1986 to 1997 and in 1990 became the founding Director of the McDonald Institute for Archaeological Research. In 1991 he was made a life peer as Baron Renfrew of Kaimsthorn, and he received the Balzan Prize in Prehistoric Archaeology for 2004. He directed new excavations on the Cycladic island of Keros from 2006 to 2008 and died on 24 November 2024, aged 87.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In Before Civilisation (1973) Renfrew used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radiocarbon-dating">radiocarbon dating</a> to challenge the assumption that prehistoric cultural innovation began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> and spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In Archaeology and Language (1987) he proposed the Anatolian hypothesis: that Proto-Indo-European, the reconstructed ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-european-languages">Indo-European languages</a>, originated about 9,000 years ago in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and spread with farming across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and into central and northern Europe. This contradicted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marija-gimbutas">Marija Gimbutas</a>'s Kurgan hypothesis, in which a migration of peoples from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic–Caspian steppe</a> about 6,000 years ago spread the language.</p><p>His use of the archaeological record to understand the ancient mind was foundational to evolutionary cognitive archaeology, and with his student Lambros Malafouris he coined the term neuroarchaeology. In 1996 he formulated the sapient paradox: why there was such a long gap between the emergence of anatomically and genetically modern humans and the development of complex behaviours. He also wrote on the ethics of looting in archaeology, in Loot, Legitimacy and Ownership (2000).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Colin Renfrew” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>One, proposed by <b>Colin Renfrew</b> and Ruth Tringham, has it develop locally from the Starčevo culture; but the earliest southern sites seem as early as the northern ones and lack local continuity.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vinca-culture">Vinča culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>David Anthony regards the Pontic–Caspian steppe as the strongest candidate for the homeland of Proto-Indo-European, with the possible exception of the Anatolian languages, while <b>Colin Renfrew</b> has argued for a Near Eastern origin.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yamnaya-culture">Yamnaya culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Colin Renfrew</b> argued in 1973 that the great monuments of Neolithic Wessex, Stonehenge among them, were raised by labour that chiefs mobilised from wide territories, and the mounds and plazas of Cahokia were built in the same way.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Stockton-on-Tees, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.57,-1.32&amp;year=1993" aria-label="Stockton-on-Tees on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Colin Renfrew"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4935 5463h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4935 5463h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.57,-1.32&amp;year=1993">What was here in 1993? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
